  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The inequality $x<-2$ corresponds to all numbers less than $-2.$ A number line for this interval is shown below:\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14191214/assets/d142ec418d86ccfce8f701ca19384d599ed2b5f7072d5ae84dcb3796baa73261.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFrom the segment on the number line, we have the following:\n\n- There is no left endpoint - it continues forever. So, we write the interval with negative infinity as the left endpoint, not included.\n\n- The right endpoint $-2$ is *not* included in the interval.\n\nTherefore, using interval notation, we represent $x<-2$ as\n\n$$\nx\\in (-\\infty ,-2).\n$$",
